WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.120
Before he was the director of Man of Steel
and Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice, Zack

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.920
Snyder weaved worlds within worlds in Sucker
Punch.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.740
Its twisted final act that was left open to
interpretation and deemed incomprehensible

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.760
by many, but we’re here to help with the
ending of Sucker Punch explained.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.560
Whose story is this?

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.300
One of the key points of contention in understanding
Sucker Punch is determining whose point of

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.400
view the story is actually being told from.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.020
The first and most straightforward idea is
that this is a story about Babydoll, who's

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.280
locked in an asylum and teams up with four
friends to escape.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.840
In the process, she imagines herself as a
burlesque dancer, and her "dances" within

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.100
the fantasy as elaborate battle sequences.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.780
Babydoll doesn't escape, in the end, but her
fantasy helps tell the story of how she got

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.980
her friend Sweet Pea out in her place.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:51.840
The second interpretation posits that all
of this happened as well, except the fantasy

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.800
is in Sweet Pea's head, as she imagines her
friends who saved her as great warriors in

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.660
an epic story.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.050
The third, and most complex, is that almost
nothing in the film "really" happened, and

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.320
that it's all a fantasy unfolding in Sweet
Pea's head as she's about to be lobotomized.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:11.440
When watching the film, it's an interesting
exercise to keep all three possibilities in

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:11.940
your head.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.360
All the world's a stage

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:18.680
Though many of the fantasy visuals in Sucker
Punch are there simply because they look cool,

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.420
a few of the details hold a deeper meaning,
including the very first thing the film shows

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:23.920
us.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.160
Snyder opens by showing us a child's bedroom
set on a stage, then dissolves that set into

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.440
Babydoll's bedroom during the death of her
mother.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.250
When Babydoll arrives at the asylum, we see
another stage in the room where Dr. Gorski

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.890
encourages her patients to act out their trauma.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.520
"This is what we call the theater.”

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:46.890
It's there, sitting on the stage on a bed
with a striking resemblance to that first

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.180
shot of Babydoll, that we see Sweet Pea for
the first time.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.860
It's also there that Dr. Gorski tells Sweet
Pea she's in control.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.900
The recurring motif of the stage plays heavily
into the film's running theme of performance

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.960
as salvation, particularly when Babydoll "dances"
to get what she needs to escape.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.200
But what if it also signals to us that this
is all part of Sweet Pea controlling her little

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.400
world, including the opening sequence in Babydoll's
bedroom?

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.500
What if that's Sweet Pea reliving her own
trauma, while the club fantasy is Sweet Pea

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.440
working through it?

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.200
Teenage Lobotomy

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.940
The "real world" of Sucker Punch seems to
show us only one lobotomy: Babydoll's, with

00:02:24.941 --> 00:02:28.010
the prelude in the opening minutes and the
aftermath coming at the end.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:33.090
There is, however, a second lobotomy sequence
at the very beginning of the club fantasy,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:37.680
which Sweet Pea interrupts to argue against
performing a mock lobotomy onstage, and refers

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.540
to herself as the star of the show.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.680
Once again, we’re offered two versions of
the same story.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.980
We can choose to believe that this is the
story of Babydoll, that she created the fantasy

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.200
world, and that her lobotomy puts a stop to
it as she sacrifices herself to allow Sweet

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.400
Pea to escape the asylum.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.580
That's a simple enough answer, but it's not
the only one.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:01.280
By showing us Sweet Pea stopping her own lobotomy,
the film offers up the idea that she is, at

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.900
least in her own mind, changing the way her
story is told.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.340
Instead of a mental patient going under the
knife, she's a warrior fighting to be free,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:12.280
and the wig is a hint that Babydoll is in
fact not a separate person.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.920
Instead, she’s a rebellious persona Sweet
Pea has created as a coping mechanism.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.620
Though she's ultimately lobotomized anyway,
Sweet Pea is "free" because the evil Blue

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.060
can't reach the real her anymore.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.200
A tale of two sisters

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.020
The parallels in Babydoll and Sweet Pea’s
stories continue with the revelation that

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.890
Rocket is Sweet Pea's sister, and Sweet Pea
is in the club, and by extension the asylum,

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.820
because she went after Rocket when she ran
away from home.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.000
Babydoll was committed to the asylum in the
first place by her violent, money-grubbing

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.030
stepfather because she accidentally killed
her own little sister while trying to save

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.640
her, which he then used as the pretense to
have her locked up.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.650
In other words, both Babydoll and Sweet Pea
got where they are because they went after

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:55.440
their sisters.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:04:00.260
Sweet Pea even loses her own sister when Rocket
sacrifices herself to save Sweet Pea’s life.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.200
The parallels are all there, bringing Sweet
Pea and Babydoll closer together, and perhaps

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.160
indicating that they’re one and the same.

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:11.879
This might even explain why Dr. Gorski never
mentions Rocket’s death, or the deaths of

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.180
Amber and Blondie: they’re imaginary.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.080
The Wise Man

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.039
Virtually every character in Sucker Punch
with a speaking role exists both as a persona

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:26.449
in the club fantasy and as a person in the
asylum, with one very notable exception: The

00:04:26.449 --> 00:04:27.120
Wise Man.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:32.120
He first appears in Babydoll's Japanese battle
sequence and exists solely in the fight scenes

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.520
until the ending, in which he's revealed to
be driving the bus that takes Sweet Pea west.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.600
We're never given any context for who the
Wise Man really is, but he only appears in

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:46.229
the "real" world after Sweet Pea is free of
the asylum and the fantasies concocted there,

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:49.380
which leads to two different interpretations
of his place in the story.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.240
Sweet Pea's narration throughout the film,
suggests that she's telling the story from

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:53.740
the future.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.720
If that’s the case, the Wise Man is just
a kindly bus driver she met during her journey

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.220
to freedom.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.180
The other possibility is that he's an invention
of her mind, a kind and nurturing man who

00:05:04.189 --> 00:05:08.280
speaks in fatherly platitudes dreamed up by
a young woman who's never known anything but

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.300
cruelty from men.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.400
A one-way trip to Paradise

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.279
In the final scene of the movie, we get one
last clue to the mystery of whether all of

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.140
this is happening in Sweet Pea’s head, or
if she really did escape the asylum.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.600
As the bus she’s riding on drives away,
it passes a billboard for the "Paradise Diner."

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.559
Back when Babydoll arrives at the asylum,
Blue mentions that’s exactly where she’ll

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:31.599
be after her lobotomy.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.140
“There won't be any of that for this one.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.120
She’ll be in paradise, if you know what
I mean.”

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.180
If you believe this is all happening in Sweet
Pea's head, the reference to Paradise could

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.089
mean that Sweet Pea herself is somewhere in
her own mind where she's finally free, after

00:05:47.089 --> 00:05:51.080
the Babydoll persona she created sacrificed
herself through the lobotomy.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:55.219
Or, perhaps, Sweet Pea is just remembering
her friend, and heading off into the sunset

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.710
to live a full life of paradise on her behalf.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.659
We’ve gone through the clues, but it’s
up to you to decide for yourself.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:02.820
“It’s like we talk about.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.340
You control this world.”

